{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments ["<b>And he planted an eshel.</b> The name of a tree. And therefore it says ויטע — 'and he planted' — for since he had a well there, he wanted to plant trees and orchards there, to irrigate them from the water of the well he had dug there, so that he and the guests coming to his house might benefit from the fruits. And because the exertion of the righteous in planting is nothing but preparation for intellectual endeavors, Scripture immediately continues: <b>and he called there on the name of HaShem.</b>" "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> ויטע אשל — א (alef) = אכילה (eating), ש (shin) = שכיבה (lodging), ל (lamed) = לויה (accompanying). And these are the very letters of שא״ל. For Avraham would say to every passerby: שאל — 'Ask' — what shall I give you, and he would give him whatever he asks." "<b>And know</b> that the mitzvah of לויה (accompanying) is the consummation of the mitzvah of hosting guests and the culmination of its reward. Thus it is written (Genesis 18): ״ואברהם הולך עמם לשלחם״, which is rendered (by Onkelos): ואברהם הוה אזיל עמהון לאלואיהון — 'and Avraham was going with them to accompany them.' And Scripture immediately attaches to it: ״וה׳ אמר״ — 'and HaShem said' — for in the merit of accompanying, the divine speech came to him to reveal the matter of Sedom. Thus too it is written (Psalms 135): ״כי יעקב בחר לו יה״ — read: בחר לויה — 'He chose accompanying.' And likewise it is alluded to in the words of Eliezer when he said: ״אל תאחרו אותי וה׳ הצליח״ and so on. And the measure of לויה I have written in connection with the verse ״ואברהם הולך עמם לשלחם.״ And in the Masorah there are two instances of ויטע in the Torah at the beginning of a verse: one — (Genesis 2) ״ויטע גן בעדן מקדם״; the second — in Avraham. And this comes to teach you that whoever maintains the mitzvah of hosting guests inherits גן עדן." "<b>And he called</b> there on the name of HaShem — an expression of calling out and proclamation. Thus too: ״יקרא איש חסדו״ — 'a man will proclaim His kindness'; ״כי שם ה׳ אקרא״ — 'for the name of HaShem I proclaim.' And the intent of Scripture is that Avraham proclaims and makes known to all creatures that the world is created anew, and that it has a Guide who is one, unique, and eternal (קדמון). Therefore it says that this proclamation was in the name of HaShem, who is One, El עולם — the eternal El — whose existence and divinity preceded the world; and that is the meaning of El עולם — namely, that He preceded (קדם) the world. Or <b>ויקרא</b> may mean 'and he prayed,' as in the view of Onkelos, peace be upon him, and it is from the language of (Psalms 30): ״אליך ה׳ אקרא.״"], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {3 "Citation of Psalms 135:3 read as 'בחר לויה' ('He chose accompanying') follows the rabbinic wordplay tradition (cf. Sotah 14a); the printed text's 'קרי בחר לויה' appears to mark this as a read-this-as tradition."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:14:13.192665412Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}